      "text": "We start with the given expression:\n\\[\n\\frac{x^4}{x^8 + 2x^6 - 4x^4 + 8x^2 + 16}\n\\]\n\nTo maximize this expression, we can use a substitution and symmetry. Let's denote:\n\\[\ny = x^2\n\\]\nThen the expression becomes:\n\\[\n\\frac{y^2}{y^4 + 2y^3 - 4y^2 + 8y + 16}\n\\]\n\nWe need to find the maximum value of this expression as \\(y\\) ranges over all non-negative real numbers. To do this, we can take the derivative of the function and set it to zero. First, let's denote the function we need to maximize as:\n\\[\nf(y) = \\frac{y^2}{y^4 + 2y^3 - 4y^2 + 8y + 16}\n\\]\n\nUsing the quotient rule, we have:\n\\[\nf'(y) = \\frac{(2y)(y^4 + 2y^3 - 4y^2 + 8y + 16) - (y^2)((4y^3 + 6y^2 - 8y + 8))}{(y^4 + 2y^3 - 4y^2 + 8y + 16)^2}\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the numerator:\n\\[\nf'(y) = \\frac{2y^5 + 4y^4 - 8y^3 + 16y^2 - y^2(4y^3 + 6y^2 - 8y + 8)}{(y^4 + 2y^3 - 4y^2 + 8y + 16)^2}\n\\]\n\\[\n= \\frac{2y^5 + 4y^4 - 8y^3 + 16y^2 - 4y^5 - 6y^4 + 8y^4 - 8y^3 y}{(y^4 + 2y^3 - 4y^2 + 8y + 16)^2}\n\\]\n\\[\n= \\frac{-2y^5 - 2y^4 - 16y^3 + 16y^2}{(y^4 + 2y^3 - 4y^2 + 8y + 16)^2}\n\\]\n\\[\n= \\frac{-2y^2(y^3 + y^2 + 8y - 8)}{(y^4 + 2y^3 - 4y^2 + 8y + 16)^2}\n\\]\n\nSetting the numerator to zero to find critical points:\n\\[\n-2y^2(y^3 + y^2 + 8y - 8) = 0\n\\]\n\nThis gives us two cases to consider:\n1. \\(y = 0\\)\n2. \\(y^3 + y^2 + 8y - 8 = 0\\)\n\nSince \\(y = 0\\) gives 0 in the original expression, we focus on the second case. The polynomial \\(y^3 + y^2 + 8y - 8\\) has a constant term and positive leading coefficient, indicating it does not have real zeros. However, it has roots where:\n\\[\ny^3 + y^2 + 8y - 8 = (y - 2)(y^2 + 4y - 4) = 0\n\\]\n\nSo, the real solutions are:\n\\[\ny = 2, \\quad y = \\sqrt{-1 + 4} = \\sqrt{3}, \\quad y = -\\sqrt{-1 + 4} = -\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\n\nWe now evaluate \\(f(y)\\) at the critical points:\n1. \\(f(0) = \\frac{0^2}{0^4 + 2 \\cdot 0^3 - 4 \\cdot 0^2 + 8 \\cdot 0 + 16} = 0\\)\n2. \\(f(2) = \\frac{2^2}{2^4 + 2 \\cdot 2^3 - 4 \\cdot 2^2 + 8 \\cdot 2 + 16} = \\frac{4}{16 + 16 - 16 + 16 + 16} = \\frac{4}{48} = \\frac{1}{12}\\)\n3. \\(f(\\sqrt{3}) = \\frac{(\\sqrt{3})^2}{(\\sqrt{3})^4 + 2(\\sqrt{3})^3 - 4(\\sqrt{3})^2 + 8(\\sqrt{3}) + 16} = \\frac{3}{9 + 6\\sqrt{3} - 12 + 8\\sqrt{3} + 16} = \\frac{3}{1\\sqrt{3} + 12} \\approx \\frac{3}{13.66} = \\frac{72}{136} \\approx 0.52\\]\n4. \\(f(-\\sqrt{3})\\) is the same as \\(f(\\sqrt{3})\\) due to symmetry.\n\nThus, the maximum value of the expression is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{12}}\n\\]",
